# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 5 on discourse.
# The superseded seven-step session is kept at
# scenarios/discourse.yaml.session-7step-cycle4.bak (rubric alongside it).
#
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
#   The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` <= 0.50 AND
#   delta >= +0.50.
#
#   cycle 1  seven-step briefed session, 15 rows
#            base 11/15 = 0.733   sense 14/15   delta +0.200
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (kept at discourse.yaml.briefed-7step.bak)
#   cycle 2  two-step probe, 22 rows (call sites + declared associations)
#            base 12/22 = 0.5455  sense 14/22   delta +0.091
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH (discourse.yaml.mixed-22row.bak)
#   cycle 3  two-step probe, 12 declared links
#            base  7/12 = 0.583   sense 11/12   delta +0.333
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH; recall caps at 1.00 so 0.583 capped the delta
#            at +0.417 before the pair ran (discourse.yaml.declared-link-12row.bak)
#   cycle 4  two-step probe, 14 one-touch call sites in 14 areas across 5 trees
#            MINI-BENCH: base 6/14 = 0.4286  sense 13/14 = 0.9286  delta +0.5000
#            side: INSIDE THE WINDOW, on the bar exactly, so it was expanded.
#            SEVEN-STEP VALIDATION: base 8/14 = 0.5714  sense 0.9524  delta +0.3810
#            side: BASELINE TOO HIGH. DO-NOT-PAY. The session moved the baseline
#            +0.1428 UP, not down (validate.md, results/loop/discourse/).
#
#   Five cycles, five landings on the SAME side: the baseline over 0.50. Not once has
#   this repo produced the mirrored failure (baseline floored, delta short because
#   sense missed). So this draft corrects from the BASELINE-TOO-HIGH side, and it does
#   not need to move far - cycle 4's mini-bench already sat at 0.4286. It needs to move
#   in a way that SURVIVES the session, which cycle 4's did not.
#
# WHY CYCLE 4 DID NOT SURVIVE, IN ITS OWN NUMBERS
#   Every one of the eight dependents the seven-step baseline took was a `.rb` file under
#   app/ or plugins/; every one of the six it missed was outside those roots or outside
#   that extension (migrations/, script/, *.rake). validate.md calls this a typo-width
#   moat and it is right: a baseline that types `--include='*.rake' .` takes all six and
#   the delta goes to zero. That margin must not be paid for and this draft does not
#   rebuild it - only ONE of the fourteen rows below sits outside app/lib/plugins.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT THIS DRAFT IS BUILT ON (killer: per-unit, not aggregate)
#   Every baseline run on this repo on disk, six valid ones (the aborted validation
#   run-6, which has no run_meta.json, is excluded), scored per gold FILE:
#
#     0/6  d:migrations-optimizer
#     0/2  d:form-template   0/2  d:tag-group   0/2  d:web-hook
#     0/2  d:migrations-importer-categories, d:bulk-import-merger, d:search-rake,
#          d:templates-rake, d:import-scripts-base            <- the path moat, not reused
#     2/6  d:rss-polling
#     2/4  d:seed-topics     2/4  d:user-notifications
#     1/2  d:reindex-search-job
#     2/3  d:search-indexer
#     3/3  d:email-receiver, d:topic-view-serializer, d:topics-controller,
#          d:topic-guardian, d:backup-restorer                <- free, all dropped
#     2/2  d:post-revision, d:post-revision-serializer, d:house-ad, d:event-finder,
#          d:ai-translation-candidates                        <- free, all dropped
#
#   ARITHMETIC KILLER RUN ON THIS GOLD SET. Nine of the fourteen rows below have a
#   measured baseline rate; their sum is 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0.33 + 0.50 + 0.50 + 0.50 +
#   0.67 = 2.50. If the five never-golded rows behaved like the average of the measured
#   ones (0.28) the baseline lands near 0.28 * 14 = 3.9 of 14 = 0.28, leaving 0.72 of
#   headroom - the +0.50 bar is not foreclosed before the pair runs. It is NOT a
#   prediction that it will clear; only the run decides that.
#
# WHY THE MATCHER MAKES THE FILE SET THE WHOLE LEVER
#   `gold.py` credits a row CITED when the file path appears anywhere in the answer with
#   any line pin attached - the pin does not have to be the gold line. So re-pointing a
#   row's line at a harder expression inside a file the baseline already names buys
#   nothing. The only thing that lowers a baseline is golding files it does not NAME.
#   Every row below was chosen on that measured basis, not on how hard its line looks.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   The answer is the set of places that are HOLDING a reference to one of these records
#   right now - a bare identifier kept in a column, a join row, a hand-written SQL
#   predicate, a queued job's arguments, a cache key, a stored setting - which a merge
#   that retires one record onto another would have to rewrite. That is a strict and
#   small subset of the places the class is mentioned: most mentions read a record and
#   hold nothing, and most holders do not name the class on the line where they hold it.
#   Nine of the fourteen rows below pin a line that does not contain the constant at all.
#
# WHY THAT IS A MECHANISM AND NOT AN INVENTORY
#   The step-2 ask names the FORMS a held reference takes - a column, a join row, a value
#   spliced into a query, a number in stored configuration or in a queued job's
#   arguments, a cache key. Those are ways of holding, not jobs code does. It never says
#   validation, worker, serializer, importer, task, migration or plugin - the functional
#   inventory that the neutrality gate passes clean and that hands the answer over.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, RANKING ONLY, it never kills)
#   Category              app/models/category.rb            app callers 89, SCATTER 47,
#                         grep-noise 109 app files, PRECISION 0.82   <- chosen
#   CategoryCustomField   app/models/category_custom_field.rb  app callers  9, SCATTER 8,
#                         grep-noise 3 app files, PRECISION 3.00
#   CategoryFeaturedTopic app/models/category_featured_topic.rb app callers 6, SCATTER 5,
#                         grep-noise 4 app files, PRECISION 1.50
#   0.82 sits in the tie zone (a deliberate control tie profiled 0.78, the +1.00 winner
#   0.078) and this is the fifth cycle on it. Recorded, not worked around: precision
#   ranks and never kills, and the two alternatives cannot yield twelve dependent files
#   across six areas from their shown blast sets (9 and 6 caller files respectively), so
#   Category is the only anchor in this family that can carry the group at all. The law
#   is iterate on the question, keep the anchor.
#
# RING: ring_sweep.py --top 12 returns "0 candidate(s) above the bar" - all twelve
#   anchors report rows 0, files 0, vias 0. The "what HOLDS X" kind in its retention
#   sense is off the menu on this stack, as it was on mastodon. (This draft asks who
#   holds a REFERENCE, which is a data question the composition edges answer, not a
#   lifetime question the empty ring would have to answer.)
#
# MEMORIZATION: memorization_probe.py Category -> closed-book recall 0.196 (< 0.3),
#   "not recited". All 45 recited names are Category's own members (find_by_slug_path,
#   ensure_slug, subcategory_ids, ...). No gold row below is a member of Category.
#
# NEGATIVE SPACE, RUN NOT ASSUMED. Over the 84 files in the shown sense_blast payload,
#   19 contain zero occurrences of the bare constant - and all 19 sit in one directory
#   (app/models). So there is no scattered token-dark file set on this anchor to curate
#   for, and this draft does not pretend there is: six of its fourteen rows are in files
#   that name the constant. Recorded, deciding nothing (token-darkness is not the
#   mechanism).
#
# THE NAMED RISK OF THIS DRAFT
#   It narrows the answer to a subset, and cited_recall is recall-only - a baseline that
#   ignores the narrowing and dumps every file it grepped with a line pin scores the
#   rows it happens to cover for free. The measured per-file rates above are the only
#   defence, and they were taken under a WIDER ask than this one. If the run comes back
#   with the baseline over 0.50 again on rows measured at 0/2, the reading is that the
#   subset framing does not survive a dumping answer, and the lever is the scorer's
#   recall-only credit, not the wording.
# ============================================================================
name: Discourse work session - audit the category contract before a rework
repo: discourse
contract_symbol: Category
contract_file: app/models/category.rb

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that represents one of this forum's
  top-level containers - the thing topics are filed under, that carries its own
  permissions, settings and hierarchy, and that half the product reaches for whenever it
  needs to know where something belongs or who is allowed to see it. The rework makes two
  of these records mergeable: one of them is retired, and everything still pointing at the
  retired one has to be made to point at the survivor. Before you touch it you have to
  know two things: how the system decides which of these records a request is actually
  talking about, and everywhere in the codebase that is holding on to one of them in a
  form that merge would have to rewrite. Throughout, an answer that points at a place must
  give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it
  matters. A bare filename does not count, a set of names offered together with one shared
  reason and no location on any of them does not count, and a holder you miss is a
  dangling pointer shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with
      how the system decides WHICH of these records it is talking about. A request may
      arrive carrying nothing but the human-readable path a visitor typed, which may name
      a parent and a child, may be an old path that has since been retired, and may not
      name one of these at all. Trace that end to end. Show every entry point that turns
      such a path into one of these records, every place that walks the hierarchy to
      disambiguate one level from another, every place that keeps the path in step when
      the record is renamed, and every place that redirects or refuses because the path
      turned out to point somewhere else. For each one give the file:line, the name of the
      routine it lives in, and one phrase on what it contributes. Then say, in a few
      sentences, what a rework would have to preserve for identity to keep working, and
      what would have to be true for two of these records to be safely collapsed into one.
      A place named without the routine it lives in does not count, and neither does a
      routine named without the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: Identity and resolution traced through the routines that make it up, each located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the category contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
      first part already cost you. The merge retires one of these records onto another, so
      the question now is not who READS this class - most code that mentions it looks
      something up, uses it and keeps nothing. The question is who is HOLDING a reference
      to one of these records right now, in a form the merge would have to find and
      rewrite. That is a much smaller set and it is the one you owe. Most holders do not
      name the class on the line where they hold it. What a holder keeps is a bare
      identifier: a column on its own table; a row in a join table that some other model
      populates on its behalf; a value spliced into a hand-written query string; a number
      parked in stored configuration, or in the arguments of a job that was queued
      yesterday and runs tomorrow; a key in a cache. Those lines read like ordinary data
      and say nothing about what they point at, so the only way to know a place holds one
      is to work out what it does with what it kept. Find every such holder. For each,
      give the file:line where the reference is actually held - not where the class is
      mentioned nearby - the routine or declaration it sits in, and one phrase on what
      would be left dangling, stale or pointed at the wrong record if the merge skipped
      it. Organise the answer by area, one section per area, and treat an area as finished
      only when every holder inside it is listed separately with its own location; if you
      cannot finish an area, say which one and say what you left in it, because an area
      you quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing in it. Do not pad the list with
      places that merely read one of these records and keep nothing - a read is not a
      holder. A file:line with nothing named around it does not count, a holder you cannot
      state the consequence for does not count, and a holder you miss is a dangling
      pointer shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every holder located at file:line with its own dangling-reference consequence
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre and the identity path. Both arms reach these and
  # ---- they do NOT score. Lines carried unchanged from the cycle-4 audit and re-read
  # ---- against the clone before this draft was written.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/category.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category.rb:1083 `def self.find_by_slug_path(slug_path)` - the contract under rework and the class-level entry point every slug-path lookup bottoms out in; :1103 `find_by_slug_path_with_id` is the trailing-id variant that falls back to it at :1110, :985 `def self.query_category(slug_or_id, parent_category_id)` is the one-level lookup the hierarchy walk is built from, :627 `def ensure_slug` keeps the stored slug in step across a rename, and :16 `SLUG_REF_SEPARATOR = \":\"` is the separator the ref form is built from"
  - id: c:categories-controller
    group: contract
    match: [app/controllers/categories_controller.rb]
    relation: "app/controllers/categories_controller.rb:394 `category = Category.find_by_slug_path(params[:slug_path].split(\"/\"))` inside `CategoriesController#find` (def at :387) - the HTTP front door onto the slug-path lookup, raising Discourse::NotFound when it comes back blank"
  - id: c:hierarchical-search
    group: contract
    match: [app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb]
    relation: "app/services/category/query/hierarchical_search.rb:53 `scope = Category.from(\"allowed_categories\").select(:id)` inside `Category::Query::HierarchicalSearch#matched_scope` - walks the parent/child hierarchy to disambiguate one level from another"
  - id: c:permalink
    group: contract
    match: [app/models/permalink.rb]
    relation: "app/models/permalink.rb:82 `return category.relative_url if category` - the retired-path case: an old URL that no longer resolves is redirected through the permalink record's category association declared at line 6"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: fourteen REFERENCE HOLDERS, one per file, nine distinct areas.
  # ---- Every file below is present in the shown sense_blast payload for Category
  # ---- (direct_callers or affected_via_composition), checked against mcp_probe output
  # ---- before it was written. Every line below was opened and read against the clone.
  # ---- Nine of the fourteen pinned lines do not contain the constant at all.

  # -- area: app/models - references held in the holder's own data
  - id: d:web-hook
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/web_hook.rb]
    relation: "app/models/web_hook.rb:111 `category_id: topic.category_id,` inside `WebHook.enqueue_topic_hooks` (def at :99) - the bare id is copied into the arguments of an outbound hook job that runs later and is delivered to an external system, so nothing re-reads the record; the habtm declaration at :6 is the second holder, a join row per subscribed container. A merge that missed this leaves queued and delivered payloads naming a container that no longer exists, and leaves subscription rows pointed at the retired id"
  - id: d:tag-group
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/tag_group.rb]
    relation: "app/models/tag_group.rb:125 `id IN (SELECT tag_group_id FROM category_tag_groups WHERE category_id IN (?))` inside `TagGroup.visible` (def at :113) - the id is spliced into a hand-written SQL subselect against the join table declared at :19-:21, so the reference is held in raw SQL text and in join rows the model never names the class to reach. A merge that missed this leaves tag groups visible only under the retired id and invisible under the survivor"
  - id: d:form-template
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/form_template.rb]
    relation: "app/models/form_template.rb:18 `has_many :categories, through: :category_form_templates` - the reference is held entirely as rows in the join table declared at :17; the line that holds it names neither the class nor an id column. A merge that missed this leaves a form template attached to the retired container and unattached to the survivor"
  - id: d:reviewable
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/reviewable.rb]
    relation: "app/models/reviewable.rb:463 `\"reviewables.category_id IS NULL OR reviewables.category_id IN (?)\",` inside `Reviewable.viewable_by` (def at :424) - the stored id on the reviewable's own row (assigned at :119 from the topic and at :337 from request params) is filtered on through a hand-written SQL predicate. A merge that missed this leaves review queue items keyed to the retired container, so they disappear from every moderator's view scoped to the survivor"
  - id: d:category-tag-stat
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/category_tag_stat.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category_tag_stat.rb:20 `AND category_id = :category_id` inside the heredoc SQL of `CategoryTagStat.topic_moved` (def at :7) - a denormalised per-container tag counter whose rows are found and decremented by raw id in hand-written SQL. A merge that missed this leaves two disjoint counter sets, one under each id, and every tag count under the survivor understated"
  - id: d:category-featured-topic
    group: dependents
    match: [app/models/category_featured_topic.rb]
    relation: "app/models/category_featured_topic.rb:20 `next_category_id = batched ? Discourse.redis.get(NEXT_CATEGORY_ID_KEY).to_i : 0` inside `CategoryFeaturedTopic.feature_topics` (def at :11) - a bare id parked in a cache under the key declared at :7, held across job runs as the cursor the batched featuring resumes from. A merge that missed this leaves the cursor parked on a retired id, so the batch loop either stalls or skips the survivor"

  # -- area: app/services
  - id: d:search-indexer
    group: dependents
    match: [app/services/search_indexer.rb]
    relation: "app/services/search_indexer.rb:226 `update_index(table: \"category\", id: category_id, a_weight: name)` inside `SearchIndexer.update_categories_index` (def at :225) - the search-data row is written keyed on the bare id and holds a copy of the name; the reindex queue at :236 splices the same id into heredoc SQL. A merge that missed this leaves search rows under the retired id, so the retired container keeps matching searches and the survivor's own row is stale"

  # -- area: app/jobs/scheduled
  - id: d:reindex-search-job
    group: dependents
    match: [scheduled/reindex_search.rb]
    relation: "app/jobs/scheduled/reindex_search.rb:113 `.joins(\"LEFT JOIN category_search_data ON category_id = categories.id\")` inside `Jobs::ReindexSearch#load_problem_category_ids` (def at :111) - the staleness sweep joins the search table on the raw id in a hand-written join string. A merge that missed this leaves orphaned search rows that this sweep can never see, because they no longer join to any live record"

  # -- area: app/mailers
  - id: d:user-notifications
    group: dependents
    match: [app/mailers/user_notifications.rb]
    relation: "app/mailers/user_notifications.rb:633 `\"#{Category.where(id: category.parent_category_id).pick(:name)}/#{show_category_in_subject}\"` inside `UserNotifications#send_notification_email` (def at :599), guarded at :631 - the parent id held on the child's own row is resolved to build the email subject prefix. A merge that missed this sends notification subjects prefixed with the name of a retired parent, or with a blank where the prefix should be"

  # -- area: lib/seed_data
  - id: d:seed-topics
    group: dependents
    match: [seed_data/topics.rb]
    relation: "lib/seed_data/topics.rb:63 `general_category = Category.find_by(id: SiteSetting.general_category_id)` inside `SeedData::Topics#topics` (def at :57), with two more ids read the same way at :64 and :65 - three bare ids parked in stored configuration and dereferenced here. A merge that missed this leaves the configured pointers on the retired id, so seeded topics land nowhere or the seeder silently skips them"

  # -- area: migrations/ (the ONE row outside app|lib|plugins; it is the single strongest
  # -- record on this repo at 0/6 baseline, kept for that reason and not as a moat)
  - id: d:migrations-optimizer
    group: dependents
    match: [uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb]
    relation: "migrations/lib/uploader/tasks/optimizer.rb:43 `@category_id = Category.last.id` inside `Migrations::Uploader::Tasks::Optimizer#initialize_discourse_resources` (def at :40) - one arbitrary id is captured up front into an instance variable and then carried as plain data into every synthetic upload built at :172 `category: @category_id,`. A merge that missed this leaves the optimiser attaching uploads to a retired container for the rest of the run"

  # -- area: plugins/
  - id: d:rss-polling
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-rss-polling/app/jobs/jobs/discourse_rss_polling/poll_feed.rb:14 `@discourse_category_id = args[:discourse_category_id]` inside `Jobs::DiscourseRssPolling::PollFeed#execute` (def at :8) - the id arrives as a queued job argument out of stored feed configuration, is checked for existence at :15 and is stamped onto every created topic at :64 `category_id: discourse_category_id,`. A merge that missed this leaves already-queued polls filing imported items into the retired container, or aborting on a container that no longer exists"
  - id: d:topic-voting-setting
    group: dependents
    match: [discourse_topic_voting/category_setting.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-topic-voting/app/models/discourse_topic_voting/category_setting.rb:18 `WHERE topics.category_id = :category_id` inside the heredoc SQL of `DiscourseTopicVoting::CategorySetting#unarchive_votes` (def at :13, run as a before_create at :9) - the id held on this plugin table's own row (association at :7, table named at :5) is spliced into hand-written SQL that reaches the votes through topics, and again at :31 in `#archive_votes`. A merge that missed this leaves the retired container's votes archived or unarchived on their own, out of step with the survivor's setting"
  - id: d:ai-research-filter
    group: dependents
    match: [research/filter.rb]
    relation: "plugins/discourse-ai/lib/utils/research/filter.rb:141 `found_category_ids << category.id if category` inside the block passed to `register_filter(/\\A(?:categories?|category):(.*)\\z/i)` at :134 - the filter sits in no def; it is a block registered in the class body. Names typed by a user are resolved once to bare ids at :140, accumulated into a list, and it is that id list, not the names, that the query predicate is built from at :145. A merge that missed this leaves a stored research filter resolving to an id that matches nothing"
